    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old patient presents with vague, progressive shoulder pain 1 year after an anatomic total shoulder arthroplasty. Inflammatory markers are minimally elevated, and there is no frank purulence on aspiration. Intraoperative cultures eventually grow a slow-growing anaerobic Gram-positive bacillus. Which organism is most likely responsible?', '["Streptococcus pyogenes", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Staphylococcus epidermidis", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Cutibacterium acnes"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Cutibacterium (formerly Propionibacterium) acnes is a common skin flora around the shoulder and a frequent cause of indolent, chronic periprosthetic joint infections following shoulder arthroplasty. It often requires prolonged incubation (up to 14 days) to grow in culture.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 65-year-old patient presents 6 months after a posterior-stabilized total knee arthroplasty complaining of a painful catch or ''clunk'' when extending the knee from a flexed position. What is the most likely etiology of this symptom?', '["Fibrous nodule formation at the superior pole of the patella", "Polyethylene wear of the patellar button", "Infection with Staphylococcus epidermidis", "Rupture of the patellar tendon", "Aseptic loosening of the tibial component"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Patellar clunk syndrome occurs in some posterior-stabilized TKAs when a fibrous nodule forms at the superior pole of the patella. As the knee extends, this nodule catches in the intercondylar box of the femoral component and then pops out with a painful ''''clunk''''.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old patient presents with vague, progressive shoulder pain 1 year after an anatomic total shoulder arthroplasty. Inflammatory markers are minimally elevated, and there is no frank purulence on aspiration. Intraoperative cultures eventually grow a slow-growing anaerobic Gram-positive bacillus. Which organism is most likely responsible?', '["Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Streptococcus pyogenes", "Staphylococcus epidermidis", "Cutibacterium acnes"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Cutibacterium (formerly Propionibacterium) acnes is a common skin flora around the shoulder and a frequent cause of indolent, chronic periprosthetic joint infections following shoulder arthroplasty. It often requires prolonged incubation (up to 14 days) to grow in culture.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old patient undergoes a total hip arthroplasty via an anterior approach. Postoperatively, the patient has profound weakness in knee extension and decreased sensation over the anterior thigh. Which structure was most likely injured during the procedure?', '["Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve", "Obturator nerve", "Sciatic nerve", "Femoral nerve", "Superior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The femoral nerve provides motor innervation to the quadriceps (knee extension) and sensory innervation to the anterior thigh. It can be injured during an anterior approach to the hip due to retractor placement over the anterior rim of the acetabulum.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old female undergoes a total hip arthroplasty via a standard anterior approach. Postoperatively, the patient is at highest risk for dislocation if the hip is placed in which of the following positions?', '["Extension, abduction, and internal rotation", "Flexion, adduction, and external rotation", "Extension and internal rotation", "Extension and external rotation", "Flexion, abduction, and external rotation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In a anterior approach to the hip, the typical position of instability causing dislocation is extension and external rotation. The Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve is also at risk during this approach. Patients are routinely placed on specific hip precautions to avoid these extreme ranges of motion early in the postoperative period.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old patient is undergoing a total joint arthroplasty. The surgical team is reviewing the risk of perioperative systemic complications. Regarding myocardial infarction, which of the following is most accurate in this setting?', '["It is completely preventable with prophylactic antibiotics", "Within the first 30 days postoperatively", "It is solely caused by the type of implant bearing surface used", "It is a rare complication seen almost exclusively in young patients", "It only occurs after the 3-month postoperative mark"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Myocardial infarction is a known systemic complication following major joint arthroplasty. Specifically, it is within the first 30 days postoperatively. Proper medical optimization and perioperative care are crucial for risk mitigation.');
